    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Output", "Typesetting", "Professional"],
    "overview": "The Linotype CRTronic is an all-in-one digital phototypesetting workstation introduced by Mergenthaler Linotype around 1979 (marketed through the 1980s). It combined a movable QWERTY keyboard, a 9-inch tiltable green-phosphor CRT, file storage, and a CRT-based imaging engine in a single desktop cabinet. Unlike punched-tape phototypesetting, composition was done interactively on screen: the operator typed, set parameters live, corrected, and exposed the job directly to photographic paper or film.\n\nIts most unusual interface feature is the analog line-length-remainder light bar: the screen renders the amount of column space remaining as a graphical light-bar gauge rather than a numeric readout — a quasi-instrumented, analog-style feedback about an inherently text-processing task. The keyboard offered 14 programmable memory keys in three levels for multicodes, and the system supported up to 97 user-defined formats with nesting, foreground AND background hyphenation/justification, and 296 files per dual mini-floppy diskette. The CRTronic family continued through the CRTronic 360 and later models, with the name carried well into the 1980s.",
    "deepDive": [
      {"heading": "The interaction", "text": "The operator sits at one cabinet containing the movable keyboard, the 9-inch tiltable CRT, the floppy storage, and the imaging engine. Composition is interactive: typesetting parameters are displayed in the text itself, and the operator sees line breaks, measure, indentation, and tab columns live, editing with a full cursor (home, next word, previous paragraph, foreground/background). The analog light-bar gauge showing remaining line length is the oddest detail — an instrument-like feedback channel in a text editor."},
      {"heading": "The keyboard", "text": "A movable keyboard with 14 programmable memory keys in 3 levels (for multicodes), assignable directly from the keyboard. Combined with 97 user-defined formats that could be nested and macro characters, the CRTronic gave a typographer a compact, programmable command surface — a step beyond standard function keys."},
      {"heading": "Legacy", "text": "The CRTronic line made digital typesetting affordable and desktop-sized, competing with Compugraphic and Monotype. The interaction model — interactive CRT composition with analog feedback — is a distinct corner of professional creative HCI, far from the big-screen graphic workstations in the museum. A period Mergenthaler brochure is preserved on the Internet Archive and the system is documented in the Smithsonian NMAH Mergenthaler Linotype Company Records."}
